title: The Imperial Proclamation
subtitle: Generous terms in one hand, a rearmament budget in the other...
new-page: true
view-if: year = 1931 and month >= 2 and not imperial_proclamation_seen
on-arrival: imperial_proclamation_seen = 1
max-visits: 1
priority: 2
tags: event

= The Imperial Proclamation

The Imperial chancery has published what it calls the Proclamation of Reunion, and the terms are generous by any reading. Any former territory returning to the Empire is promised its own diet, its own courts, its language and religion left alone, and its debts assumed by the Imperial treasury. The clause every newspaper in the capital reprinted promises that its labour law will be left exactly as its own legislature writes it.

The same week's Imperial budget provides for eleven new regiments, two shipyards, and a doubling of the Northern Fleet.

The <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Axton Empire**</span> had no money in Vera when the Exchange fell. Its workshops never stopped and its yards never emptied. While every other treasury on the continent spent the last year propping up its banks, the Empire has been buying steel. <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**Ethia**</span> has protested. The <span style="color: #708090;">**Allied States**</span> have protested. Nobody has done anything, because nobody is in a position to.

The proclamation is already circulating in the Commonwealth's own marches, in a good translation.

- @suppress
- @stand_firm
- @generous_terms
- @counter_propaganda

@suppress
title: Suppress this propaganda!
subtitle: Use the ministries we hold. The proclamation does not circulate here.
view-if: chancellor_party == "Marcher" or interior_minister_party == "Marcher" or justice_minister_party == "Marcher"
on-arrival: reformist_dissent += 5; knight_dissent += 5

Presses are seized, the translation is banned from the post, and the men distributing it in the marches spend a night in the cells. The proclamation stops circulating, and the Empire's offer never reaches most of the people it was written for. The <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Royalists**</span> gain nothing from an argument that never takes place.

Our own reformists are uneasy. They spent a decade arguing that a republic answers speech with speech, and now they are asked to explain why this speech was different. Reformist and Knight dissent rise.

@stand_firm
title: Stand firm against the Empire.
subtitle: Answer the proclamation in public, whatever the terms.
on-arrival: reformist_dissent += 5; knight_dissent += 5; dufour_angry += 5; commons_royalist += 3; burgher_royalist += 2; guild_royalist += 2; faithful_royalist += 2; disfavored_royalist += 2

We answer it directly. The Commonwealth is not a territory to be reacquired on favourable terms, and no diet the Empress grants can be worth the sovereignty it replaces. This is the right answer and a poor tactic, because every hour spent contesting the proclamation is an hour in which the proclamation is discussed.

The <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Royalists**</span> could not have asked for better. They did not have to defend the terms, since we did that for them by attacking them, and their meetings are noticeably fuller afterward. Their support rises across every class. President DuFour, who would have preferred the whole business handled quietly, is displeased.

Our reformists think the tone needlessly absolute for a country that will still have to trade with the Empire next year, and dissent among them rises.

@generous_terms
title: Admit that the terms are generous.
subtitle: Say what half the country is already thinking. The Left will not forgive it.
on-arrival: left_dissent += 8; center_dissent += 8; dufour_angry += 5; empire_posture += 4 if empire_posture <= 96; commons_royalist += 3; burgher_royalist += 2; guild_royalist += 2; faithful_royalist += 2; disfavored_royalist += 2

We decline to pretend the terms are worse than they are. Read plainly, they concede more to a returning province than the Commonwealth's own constitution concedes to its marches, and we say so.

Our centrists and our Left consider this an act of astonishing political stupidity. To answer an annexation offer by conceding that it is well drafted hands the argument to everyone who wants the annexation, and the wing of the party that has spent its life fighting the Empire says so in language that does not print well. Dissent in both factions rises sharply.

The Imperial legation is delighted, and the Empire's posture warms. So are the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Royalists**</span>, who now have a Reformist quotation for their pamphlets, and their support rises. President DuFour is displeased.

@counter_propaganda
title: Release counter-propaganda.
subtitle: -1 resources. Fight the pamphlet with a pamphlet.
choose-if: resources >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: Not enough resources.
on-arrival: resources -= 1; royalist_urgency += 1 if neorevisionism == 0

We answer print with print. Our own presses run day and night on the Empire's labour record, its conscription rolls, and the diets it granted the last time it promised one. The two pamphlets cancel each other out almost exactly, which is the most that can be said for the effort.

[? if neorevisionism == 0: The exercise does teach a handful of our organisers how much better the other side is at this than we are, and they begin asking whether a party that only argues is equipped for the decade ahead. The Royalists' confidence grows. ?][? if neorevisionism > 0: Our neorevisionists ran the campaign and are quietly satisfied with it, which is more than anyone else in the party can say. ?]
